<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-ascorbate biosynthesis II (plants, L-gulose pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY4FS-11" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: ASCORBATE| (ascorbic acid, vitamin C) is a reducing agent and antioxidant biosynthesized by plants, eukaryotic algae, some bacteria and many, but not all vertebrates.|FRAME: ASCORBATE| is an essential vitamin in the diet of humans, non-human primates and certain other vertebrates that cannot biosynthesize it. These species rely on mechanisms for efficient uptake and recycling of this vitamin (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-6370|). A deficiency of vitamin C leads to the disorder scurvy. |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is used in the pharmaceutical industry for the production of vitamin supplements, cosmetics and therapeutic preparations. It is also used in the food, beverage and animal feed industries. Reviewed in |CITS: [16516325] [17222174]|. In photosynthesizing plants and eukaryotic algae |FRAME: ASCORBATE| has roles in metabolism, electron transport, the cell cycle and responses to pathogens and abiotic stress. When coupled with its redox system, the ascorbate/glutathione cycle, |FRAME: ASCORBATE| can effectively regulate cellular |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| levels (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-2261|) |CITS: [18460785]|. Interestingly, |FRAME: TAX-1117| do not possess any of the known enzymes for ascorbate biosynthesis, and minimize photo-oxidative stress using alternative mechanisms, such as peroxiredoxins, catalases and glutathione peroxidases, suggesting that the use of ascorbate evolved after the endosymbiotic acquisition of a cyanobacterial ancestor by a non-photosynthetic eukaryote |CITS: [23109712][25768426]|. &lt;B&gt;Ascorbate Biosynthesis in Plants&lt;/B&gt; Four different pathways for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| have been described in plants. &amp;bull; The first plant pathway was suggested in 1998 by Wheeler et al |CITS: [9620799]|. The pathway starts from |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| and proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-Galactopyranose| and |FRAME: CPD-330| (see |FRAME: PWY-882|). &amp;bull; A second pathway, known as the L-gulose pathway, also starts with |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|. However, that pathway proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-gulopyranose| and |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS:[12954627]| (see |FRAME: PWY4FS-11|). &amp;bull; A third pathway was described from |FRAME: TAX-3747| (strawberry). In that pathway |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is formed from |FRAME: CPD-330|, as in the first pathway. However, the pathway starts from |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE|, and proceeds via |FRAME: CPD0-1083| |CITS:[13363799][12524550]|. Tracer and feeding studies have also shown conversion of |FRAME: CPD-23305| to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| |CITS: [Loewus63]|. (see |FRAME: PWY-8143|). &amp;bull; A fourth pathway suggested that |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL| can also be channeled towards the production of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: EC-1.13.99.1|, based on the effect of overexpressing the |FRAME: AT4G26260| gene. According to that proposal, |FRAME: GLUCURONATE| is reduced to |FRAME: L-GULONATE|, which is converted to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS: [14976233]| (see |FRAME: PWY-8142|). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: EC-5.1.3.18|, converts |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| not only to |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE|, an intermediate in the L-galactose pathway, but also to |FRAME: CPD-7078| |CITS: [12954627]|. This finding prompted the proposal of the L-gulose pathway. Furthermore, feeding Arabidopsis cells with |FRAME: L-Gulose| or |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| increased ascorbate level, supporting the existence of the L-gulose route. Initially, an argument against the existence of the gulose pathway was the lack of enzymes dedicated to catalyze the last two steps. The L-galactose dehydrogenase purified from pea was shown active with L-gulose, although the enzyme&apos;s substrate specificity with L-gulose (Km= 3.7 mM) was very low compared with its preferred substrate L-galactose (Km= 0.43 mM) |CITS:[12047629]|. The activity of |FRAME: EC-1.3.2.M1|, was reported in potato tubers, in both the mitochondrial and cytosolic cell fractions |CITS:[12954627]|. Further support was obtained with the characterization of |FRAME: GQT-4735| and |FRAME: GQT-4288|, which showed that they are specific for |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS: [20622436][30112455]|.</Description>
